<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Wallachia</h1><p class="st-meta">1310 CE – 1859 CE (approx.) · capital Bucharest</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Romanian principality between the Carpathians and the Danube, home of Mircea the Elder and Vlad the Impaler, which united with Moldavia to form Romania.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Romanian voivodeships such as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/voivodeship-of-litovoi">Voivodeship of Litovoi</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/voivodeship-of-seneslau">Voivodeship of Seneslau</a> were united under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basarab-i">Basarab I</a> in the early 14th century; tradition dates the founding to about 1310. In 1330 Basarab defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-hungary">Charles I of Hungary</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-posada">Posada</a>, securing independence.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The voivode (prince) ruled with boyars, and the throne was elective within the ruling family, which bred constant rivalry. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mircea-the-elder">Mircea the Elder</a> (1386–1418) fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rovine">Rovine</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlad-the-impaler">Vlad III</a> fought them in 1462. From the 15th century Wallachia paid tribute to the Ottomans but kept its own rulers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> church. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-the-brave">Michael the Brave</a> briefly united Wallachia, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-transylvania">Transylvania</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Moldavia</a> in 1600. From 1716 to 1821 the Ottomans appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phanariotes">Phanariot Greek</a> princes.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-adrianople-1829">Treaty of Adrianople</a> in 1829 Wallachia came under Russian protection. In 1859 both it and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Principality of Moldavia</a> elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandru-ioan-cuza">Alexandru Ioan Cuza</a> as prince, creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/united-principalities">United Principalities</a>, which became <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-romania">Romania</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-wallachia.jpg" alt="Principality of Wallachia" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Alex:D · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Wallachia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1310 CE – 1859 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bucharest</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Basarab I, Mircea the Elder, Michael the Brave</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Romanian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Wallachia, Țara Românească, Ungrovlachia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1584&amp;focus=principality-of-wallachia" aria-label="Principality of Wallachia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5304 606 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Wallachia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5624 862L5446 783M5624 862L5709 970M5624 862L6487 457M5624 862L5624 863M5624 862L5570 865"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5446 783h0M5709 970h0M6487 457h0M5624 863h0M5570 865h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5624 862h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5624 862h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1584&amp;focus=principality-of-wallachia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
